    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d231">

                <pb facs="0419" n="1005" sameAs="frus:frus1949v07p2/pg1005"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 Control (Korea)/5–349: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Korea</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Muccio</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">priority</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Seoul</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1949-05-03">May 3, 1949.</date><anchor n="1"
                            xml:id="d231-f1"/>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>483. Further to Deptel 268, April 21, I submit
                    following comment re paragraph 6 thereof.</p>
                <pb facs="0420" n="1006" xml:id="pg1006"/>
                <p>1. While I consider ROK unable economically,
                    industrially and technically to support effective air force, I feel ROK should not be denied opportunity develop some
                    knowledge and capability aviation.</p>
                <p>2. After consultation with General Roberts, military attaché, and others I
                    recommend we provide ROK with following
                    additional aircraft: (<hi rendition="#italic">a</hi>) 20 liaison craft; (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">b</hi>) 15 single engine trainers (AT–6); (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">c</hi>) 5 transports (C–47);</p>
                <p>3. Additional liaison craft requested would supplement 16 L–4’s and L–5’s now
                    operational. These craft found highly valuable for observation purposes in Cheju
                    and Chiri Mts. Operations against Commie guerrillas would also be helpful in
                    spotting smuggling and hostile infiltration activities.</p>
                <p>4. Trainers requested would be utilized to train ROK aviation personnel of which nucleus now exists. These trainers
                    would be valuable morale and psychological factors.</p>
                <p>5. Transports urgently needed for emergency transportation ROK personnel and equipment within Korea. At
                    present USAFIK furnishes emergency service
                        ROK but this will no longer exist on
                    withdrawal USAFIK. Last October, for
                    instance, US transports rushed Korean personnel, ammunition, communication
                    equipment to Yosu–Sunchon area.</p>
                <p>6. If I am able offer Rhee above requested moderate requirements, I feel it will
                    head off further ROK inordinate requests for
                    aircraft and at same time reconcile Rhee more easily to fact of US troop
                    withdrawal.</p>
                <p>Sent Dept; repeated CINCFE.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Muccio</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d231-f1" n="1" xml:id="d231-f1-content">
                <p>This message was not received in the Department until May 6, at 2:34 p. m., and
                    bore the notation that it had been delayed in transmission.</p>
            </note>
